license: "MIT"

abstract: >
  Software ecosystems are socio-technical information environments whose
  repository, contributor, dependency, and technology data are heterogeneous,
  context-dependent, and continuously evolving. This paper presents the
  Ecosystem Intelligence Transformation Framework (EITF), a design science
  framework that transforms these digital traces into reproducible, traceable
  ecosystem intelligence for human decision support. EITF is instantiated in
  SecoDash, which integrates GitHub/GitLab acquisition, 61 repository-level
  socio-technical indicators, three ecosystem-level aggregate types,
  snapshot-based provenance, knowledge graphs, dashboards, and bounded
  natural-language querying. The evaluation combines an ecosystem
  demonstration, sampling and term-level robustness analyses, and a
  15-participant user study based on the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM),
  complemented by open-ended feedback. The results show that sampling choices
  materially affect ecosystem profiles, while participants report generally
  positive perceptions of usefulness and usability. The framework provides
  reusable design knowledge for ecosystem-intelligence systems by emphasizing
  provenance, traceability, and human judgment.

